    "details": "Bruno Alla reports:\n\n> An unauthenticated attacker can defeat Django\\'s\n> \\\"DATA_UPLOAD_MAX_MEMORY_SIZE\\\" limit and force a worker process to\n> allocate arbitrary memory, by naming a compression codec in the\n> Content-Type charset parameter. A 108-byte request body produces a\n> 50,331,648-character value inside the view, an amplification factor of\n> 466,033, and grows process peak RSS by about 97 MiB. No application\n> configuration is required beyond DRF\\'s default parser set; the\n> endpoint only has to accept a JSON body.\n\n> Deleting an authentication token through \\\"TokenProxy\\\" silently\n> deletes nothing. The call raises no exception and reports success, the\n> database row survives, and the token keeps authenticating. Any\n> application, admin integration or operational script that revokes\n> credentials by calling \\\".delete()\\\" on a \\\"TokenProxy\\\" instance\n> believes it has revoked a token that is still live.\n\n> DRF\\'s content negotiation parses each Accept token several times per\n> request and never limits the number of tokens or their total size.\n> Each parse ends up in Django\\'s parse_header_parameters, which takes\n> quadratic time when a quoted parameter value is packed with\n> semicolons. On my machine a single GET with a 780KB Accept header made\n> of 96 such tokens kept one gunicorn worker busy for 18.87 seconds\n> (Django 6.1, djangorestframework 3.18.0, gunicorn 26.0.0, Python 3.13,\n> Docker on macOS). The header is syntactically valid, fits inside\n> gunicorn\\'s default header limits, and needs no authentication. Two\n> concurrent requests, which is one per sync worker, made a default two\n> worker deployment stop answering for my whole 40 second test window.\n> It recovered half a second after I stopped sending.\n",
